

Big O Notation : Measures how the performance of an algorithm changes as the input size grows.
                 It describes the worst-case scenario to understand the algorithm's efficiency.

                 How code slows as data grows



-------- Real-World Ex: Finding a Name in a Phonebook  ------------------------
    Imagine you have a big phonebook with thousands of names in alphabetical order.
    You want to find "John Doe." Let's see different ways you might search and how Big O Notation applies


    1. Constant Time - O(1)
    Ex: You know "John Doe" is on page 50, so you open page 50 directly.
    Time Taken: Instantly, no matter how big the phonebook is.
    Big O: O(1) (Fastest - time doesn’t depend on the phonebook size).


    2. Linear Time - O(n)
    Ex: You don’t know where "John Doe" is, so you check every name one by one from the first page.
    Time Taken: If there are 1,000 names, you might have to check all 1,000.
    Big O: O(n) (Time increases as the phonebook gets bigger).


    3. Logarithmic Time - O(log n)
    Ex: You open the phonebook in the middle and check the name.
    • If "John Doe" comes before the middle, search the first half.
    • If "John Doe" comes after, search the second half.
    • Keep dividing the phonebook in half until you find the name.
    Time Taken: Much faster because you reduce the search space quickly.
    Big O: O(log n) (Very efficient for large phonebooks).


    4. Quadratic Time - O(n²)
    Ex: You compare every name in the phonebook with every other name (not useful for searching, but happens in sorting tasks).
    Time Taken: If there are 1,000 names, you might have to compare 1,000 × 1,000 = 1,000,000 times!
    Big O: O(n²) (Gets very slow as the phonebook size increases).















    ------ Why It Matters -------
    Big O helps you choose the best method for tasks like searching, sorting, or calculating. In programming,
    efficient algorithms save time and computing power.